Body:
@tricktron

I see the failing Windows e2e job is not caused by this PR, it is caused by the base branch. 

What would you suggest?

`withTmp()` creates a new `Instance` for each tmp directory via `Instance.provide`, but it never disposes the instance afterwards. Since `Instance` caches contexts globally, this can leak state/resources across tests and grow the cache over the suite. Consider adding cleanup (e.g., `Instance.dispose()`/`Instance.disposeAll()`) in a `finally` or `afterEach` for these tests.
```suggestion
  try {
    await Instance.provide({
      directory: tmp.path,
      fn,
    })
  } finally {
    await Instance.disposeAll()
  }
```

`withTmp()` provisions a new cached `Instance` per tmp directory but does not dispose it afterwards. Because `Instance.provide` caches instances globally, this can leak resources/state across tests and cause the cache to grow. Consider disposing the instance in a `finally` or reintroducing an `afterEach` that calls `Instance.disposeAll()`.
```suggestion
  try {
    await Instance.provide({
      directory: tmp.path,
      fn,
    })
  } finally {
    await Instance.disposeAll()
  }
```

The tool integrity repair is only enabled for models using `@ai-sdk/anthropic` and `@ai-sdk/amazon-bedrock`. This omits the Vertex Anthropic provider (`@ai-sdk/google-vertex/anthropic` / providerID `google-vertex-anthropic`), which is treated as Anthropic elsewhere in this module and likely has the same tool-call/tool-result pairing requirement. Consider including that provider in the condition so Vertex Anthropic sessions can’t be corrupted the same way.
```suggestion
    if (
      model.api.npm === "@ai-sdk/anthropic" ||
      model.api.npm === "@ai-sdk/amazon-bedrock" ||
      model.providerID === "google-vertex-anthropic"
    ) {
```

`local.model.variant.current()` is now computed via `resolveModelVariant(...)`, which collapses the persisted selection (`selected`) and agent default (`configured`) into a single “effective” value. This is risky because other call sites (e.g., prompt submission) typically need the explicit user override (including the "default" sentinel) rather than the resolved effective variant; otherwise users can’t explicitly override an agent default back to base behavior, and the client may send agent defaults as if they were user-selected. Consider splitting this into separate APIs (e.g., keep `selected()` for request payloads, and use `effective()` for display) or otherwise preserve the "default" sentinel through the value used for network requests.
```suggestion
            return this.selected()
          },
          effective() {
            return resolveModelVariant({
              variants: this.list(),
              selected: this.selected(),
              configured: this.configured(),
            })
          },
```

`expires` is derived in a way that returns seconds (`Math.floor(ms / 1000)` and potentially raw numeric `expires_at`). Elsewhere OAuth `expires` is compared against `Date.now()` (ms), so this may be interpreted as already-expired. Normalize `expires_at` to a millisecond timestamp before storing it in the OAuth auth object.
```suggestion
    const normalizeEpochToMs = (epoch: number) => {
      return epoch < 1_000_000_000_000 ? epoch * 1000 : epoch
    }

    const value = body.expires_at
    if (typeof value === "number") return normalizeEpochToMs(value)
    if (typeof value === "string") {
      const parsed = Number.parseInt(value, 10)
      if (!Number.isNaN(parsed)) return normalizeEpochToMs(parsed)
      const ms = new Date(value).getTime()
      if (!Number.isNaN(ms)) return ms
```

The new `/:sessionID/usage` route adds non-trivial behavior (latest assistant message + summed cost) but there are existing server endpoint tests for session APIs (e.g. `packages/opencode/test/server/session-messages.test.ts`). Please add coverage for this new endpoint, including: (1) correct aggregation when assistant/user messages are mixed, (2) `last` being absent when there are no assistant messages, and (3) 404 behavior for a missing session once implemented.
```suggestion
        const sessionID = c.req.valid("param").sessionID
        const session = await Session.get(sessionID)
        if (!session) return c.notFound()
```

Consider adding `font-display: swap` (or similar) to this `@font-face` to avoid FOIT (invisible text) while the bundled TTF loads, especially on first launch.
```suggestion
  font-style: normal;
  font-display: swap;
```
